Team,

Annex putting Calthorpe Part 4 onto a single page. Use this as the
working anchor for the value-chain views in the market overview
diagram and the target assessment competitive-position section. All
figures triangulated from Calthorpe Part 4 plus public retailer
filings (Foodstuffs Own Brands tender process documentation,
Woolworths NZ supplier portal); Aurora-specific exposure figures
are Calthorpe-triangulation only and need confirmatory validation.
I've dropped the tables in as screenshots from my working file -
shout if you want the underlying numbers.

- Iris

1. unit economics per dozen - value-chain margin per stage

Anchor: NZ shell egg dozen, retail channel, NZ$ FY26.

Free-range dozen (retails NZ$8.50 at Pak'nSave / Countdown):

Barn dozen (retails ~NZ$7.00):

Colony dozen (retails ~NZ$5.60):

key point (Calthorpe verbatim, Part 4 [00:03:30]): "Retail in NZ is
structurally the highest-margin step. Producer is asset-heavy,
low-margin per dozen, capital-intensive. Retailer is asset-light...
and captures the largest unit profit. That is the value chain in
one paragraph." Retailer EBITDA per dozen runs ~2.5x producer EBITDA
per dozen on the same product.

2. foodservice + industrial channel economics

foodservice (Bidfood / Gilmours dominant distributors):

  Producer gate (barn into foodservice):    NZ$3.80 - 4.20 / dozen
  Distributor margin layer:                 +12 - 18% on gate
  Operator menu margin:                     compounds further

  Bidfood: ~24 branches nationwide, dominant FS distributor.
  Gilmours: 100% NZ-owned, Foodstuffs co-op related party.

industrial (bakeries, mayonnaise mfg, food processors, breakers):

  Producer gate (industrial shell):         NZ$3.20 - 3.60 / dozen
  Breaker / processor margin layer:         +10 - 15% on gate
  Producer EBITDA per dozen:                NZ$0.25 - 0.40 (thinnest)

  Functions as residual disposal channel for grading-line seconds
  (4-6% of throughput) more than a strategic margin channel for
  scale producers.

3. retailer structure and buying-power asymmetry

Foodstuffs - cooperative (Pak'nSave + New World):

  - two-layer buying structure. National layer = Foodstuffs Own
    Brands Limited (Pams, Pams Value, Pams Finest tenders).
    Plus FSNI / FSSI national category buying for branded SKUs.
  - Owner-operator layer = individual store owners have local
    ranging discretion within national framework. Branded SKU
    delisting at national level may still leave 30-60 stores
    stocking via owner-operator discretion.
  - Foodstuffs SME faster-payment package launched early 2026
    (30-day terms for sub-NZ$1M annual suppliers); Aurora at
    NZ$85M revenue is well above threshold and is on standard
    60-day terms.

Woolworths NZ - centralized single-corporate (Countdown rebrand):

  - single-layer buying. National buyer per category, no store-
    level discretion. One contract for all Countdown stores.
  - Private label: Woolworths house brand + Macro Free Range
    premium tier.
  - Standard payment terms: 60-90 days from invoice.
  - Higher single-counterparty risk for any supplier - one
    decision delists nationally. Calthorpe estimate: extracts
    3-5 percentage points more producer-side margin compression
    than Foodstuffs in steady state, on equivalent product spec.

category captaincy:

  Category captain status held by Mainland Poultry across both
  retailers - they advise on planograms, lead range reviews,
  present category at retailer reviews. Roughly 1-2 percentage
  points of share-of-shelf advantage from planogram influence.
  Aurora does not have category-captain status with either
  retailer. They live with the planogram Mainland helps shape.

4. Aurora retailer mix (Calthorpe part 4 triangulation)

  Foodstuffs banners (FSNI + FSSI Pak'nSave + New World):
                                       55-60% of retail volume
  Woolworths NZ Countdown:             35-40% of retail volume
  Costco NZ + independents:            residual 5-10%

  Tilt is foodstuffs-favored, which is the better commercial
  position to be in given the buying-power asymmetry above.
  Worth confirming current split in confirmatory - tender wins
  and losses move this materially.

5. private-label exposure - material margin compression

Calthorpe Part 4 [00:18:58, 00:20:40, 00:22:24, 00:37:22]
working anchor:

  Branded retail (Aurora's "Sunbright Free Range" + secondary
  barn / colony label):
    Free-range gate:                NZ$5.40 / dozen
    Barn gate:                      NZ$4.50 / dozen
    Producer EBITDA / dozen FR:     NZ$0.65 - 0.85
    Producer EBITDA / dozen barn:   NZ$0.50 - 0.65

  Private label (Pams Free Range FSOB; Macro Free Range +
  Woolworths house barn for Woolworths NZ):
    Free-range gate:                NZ$4.55 - 4.85 / dozen
                                    (12-18% lower than branded)
    Barn gate:                      NZ$3.85 - 4.10 / dozen
    Producer EBITDA / dozen FR:     NZ$0.30 - 0.45
    Producer EBITDA / dozen barn:   NZ$0.20 - 0.35

Aurora private-label exposure (Calthorpe triangulation, two
retail-buyer conversations):

  Free-range volume PL share:            ~48% (working anchor;
                                         range 40-55%)
  Barn volume PL share:                  ~55-65%
  Blended retail PL share:               ~50%

  Blended retail EBITDA per dozen at 50/50 branded/PL on free-
  range = NZ$0.575 - which is ~23% lower than a pure-branded
  model implies. Aurora's reported FY24 EBITDA margin of 12.8%
  is consistent with this private-label blend; pure-branded
  would model at 15-17%. A buyer who anchors off pure-branded
  comps will overshoot achievable margin.

trade-spend overlay:

  Branded SKU trade spend (slotting, end-cap features, multi-buy
  promo rebates): typically 4-7% of retail revenue going back
  to retailers. Already netted into reported producer gate
  numbers but not always broken out in management accounts.
  Confirmatory must request the trade-spend roll-up.

private-label tender-loss risk:

  Calthorpe Part 4 [00:38:54]: "private-label tender losses
  are the single largest revenue-cliff risk for a half-PL
  producer." Foodstuffs Own Brands tenders typically run on
  3-year cycles. Two of Aurora's PL lines come up for retender
  inside the next 24 months per Calthorpe sector contacts.
  Loss of a single Pams FR tender = approx NZ$8-12M revenue
  cliff at gate prices.

6. working-capital exposure

  Standard retailer payment terms (Aurora at NZ$85M revenue):
    Foodstuffs:                  60 days from invoice
    Woolworths NZ:               60-90 days from invoice
    Foodservice (via Bidfood):   30-45 days from invoice
    Industrial:                  45-60 days from invoice

  Producer pays feed suppliers on 30-day terms standard.
  Working-capital gap on retail revenue ~30 days of revenue.
  At Aurora's ~NZ$60M of retail revenue, receivables tied
  up = approx NZ$5M. Not catastrophic, worth modelling.

7. pricing-power reality check

Calthorpe Part 4 [00:40:24]: "#2 with 18% volume share against
a #1 at 27% means Mainland sets the price and Aurora follows.
Plus Aurora has zero category-captain status. Plus half their
retail is private-label where they have no pricing power by
definition. So 'pricing power' as a thesis pillar is - polite
version - aspirational. I'd treat the asset as a price-taker
on the volume that matters."

Sell-side framing of Aurora as price-setter is not supported
by the value-chain analysis. Treat as price-taker for modeling.

END annex

