<p>key point (Calthorpe verbatim, Part 4 [00:03:30]): &quot;Retail in NZ is structurally the highest-margin step. Producer is asset-heavy, low-margin per dozen, capital-intensive. Retailer is asset-light... and captures the largest unit profit. That is the value chain in one paragraph.&quot; Retailer EBITDA per dozen runs ~2.5x producer EBITDA per dozen on the same product.</p>
<h3>2. foodservice + industrial channel economics</h3>
<p>foodservice (Bidfood / Gilmours dominant distributors):</p>
<p>Producer gate (barn into foodservice):    NZ$3.80 - 4.20 / dozen Distributor margin layer:                 +12 - 18% on gate Operator menu margin:                     compounds further</p>
<p>Bidfood: ~24 branches nationwide, dominant FS distributor. Gilmours: 100% NZ-owned, Foodstuffs co-op related party.</p>
<p>industrial (bakeries, mayonnaise mfg, food processors, breakers):</p>
<p>Producer gate (industrial shell):         NZ$3.20 - 3.60 / dozen Breaker / processor margin layer:         +10 - 15% on gate Producer EBITDA per dozen:                NZ$0.25 - 0.40 (thinnest)</p>
<p>Functions as residual disposal channel for grading-line seconds (4-6% of throughput) more than a strategic margin channel for scale producers.</p>
<h3>3. retailer structure and buying-power asymmetry</h3>
<p>Foodstuffs - cooperative (Pak&#x27;nSave + New World):</p>
<p>- two-layer buying structure. National layer = Foodstuffs Own Brands Limited (Pams, Pams Value, Pams Finest tenders). Plus FSNI / FSSI national category buying for branded SKUs. - Owner-operator layer = individual store owners have local ranging discretion within national framework. Branded SKU delisting at national level may still leave 30-60 stores stocking via owner-operator discretion. - Foodstuffs SME faster-payment package launched early 2026 (30-day terms for sub-NZ$1M annual suppliers); Aurora at NZ$85M revenue is well above threshold and is on standard 60-day terms.</p>
<p>Woolworths NZ - centralized single-corporate (Countdown rebrand):</p>
<p>- single-layer buying. National buyer per category, no store- level discretion. One contract for all Countdown stores. - Private label: Woolworths house brand + Macro Free Range premium tier. - Standard payment terms: 60-90 days from invoice. - Higher single-counterparty risk for any supplier - one decision delists nationally. Calthorpe estimate: extracts 3-5 percentage points more producer-side margin compression than Foodstuffs in steady state, on equivalent product spec.</p>
<p>category captaincy:</p>
<p>Category captain status held by Mainland Poultry across both retailers - they advise on planograms, lead range reviews, present category at retailer reviews. Roughly 1-2 percentage points of share-of-shelf advantage from planogram influence. Aurora does not have category-captain status with either retailer. They live with the planogram Mainland helps shape.</p>
<h3>4. Aurora retailer mix (Calthorpe part 4 triangulation)</h3>
<p>Foodstuffs banners (FSNI + FSSI Pak&#x27;nSave + New World): 55-60% of retail volume Woolworths NZ Countdown:             35-40% of retail volume Costco NZ + independents:            residual 5-10%</p>
<p>Tilt is foodstuffs-favored, which is the better commercial position to be in given the buying-power asymmetry above. Worth confirming current split in confirmatory - tender wins and losses move this materially.</p>
<h3>5. private-label exposure - material margin compression</h3>
<p>Calthorpe Part 4 [00:18:58, 00:20:40, 00:22:24, 00:37:22] working anchor:</p>
<p>Branded retail (Aurora&#x27;s &quot;Sunbright Free Range&quot; + secondary barn / colony label): Free-range gate:                NZ$5.40 / dozen Barn gate:                      NZ$4.50 / dozen Producer EBITDA / dozen FR:     NZ$0.65 - 0.85 Producer EBITDA / dozen barn:   NZ$0.50 - 0.65</p>
<p>Private label (Pams Free Range FSOB; Macro Free Range + Woolworths house barn for Woolworths NZ): Free-range gate:                NZ$4.55 - 4.85 / dozen (12-18% lower than branded) Barn gate:                      NZ$3.85 - 4.10 / dozen Producer EBITDA / dozen FR:     NZ$0.30 - 0.45 Producer EBITDA / dozen barn:   NZ$0.20 - 0.35</p>
<p>Aurora private-label exposure (Calthorpe triangulation, two retail-buyer conversations):</p>
<p>Free-range volume PL share:            ~48% (working anchor; range 40-55%) Barn volume PL share:                  ~55-65% Blended retail PL share:               ~50%</p>
<p>Blended retail EBITDA per dozen at 50/50 branded/PL on free- range = NZ$0.575 - which is ~23% lower than a pure-branded model implies. Aurora&#x27;s reported FY24 EBITDA margin of 12.8% is consistent with this private-label blend; pure-branded would model at 15-17%. A buyer who anchors off pure-branded comps will overshoot achievable margin.</p>
<p>trade-spend overlay:</p>
<p>Branded SKU trade spend (slotting, end-cap features, multi-buy promo rebates): typically 4-7% of retail revenue going back to retailers. Already netted into reported producer gate numbers but not always broken out in management accounts. Confirmatory must request the trade-spend roll-up.</p>
<p>private-label tender-loss risk:</p>
<p>Calthorpe Part 4 [00:38:54]: &quot;private-label tender losses are the single largest revenue-cliff risk for a half-PL producer.&quot; Foodstuffs Own Brands tenders typically run on 3-year cycles. Two of Aurora&#x27;s PL lines come up for retender inside the next 24 months per Calthorpe sector contacts. Loss of a single Pams FR tender = approx NZ$8-12M revenue cliff at gate prices.</p>
<h3>6. working-capital exposure</h3>
<p>Standard retailer payment terms (Aurora at NZ$85M revenue): Foodstuffs:                  60 days from invoice Woolworths NZ:               60-90 days from invoice Foodservice (via Bidfood):   30-45 days from invoice Industrial:                  45-60 days from invoice</p>
<p>Producer pays feed suppliers on 30-day terms standard. Working-capital gap on retail revenue ~30 days of revenue. At Aurora&#x27;s ~NZ$60M of retail revenue, receivables tied up = approx NZ$5M. Not catastrophic, worth modelling.</p>
<h3>7. pricing-power reality check</h3>
<p>Calthorpe Part 4 [00:40:24]: &quot;#2 with 18% volume share against a #1 at 27% means Mainland sets the price and Aurora follows. Plus Aurora has zero category-captain status. Plus half their retail is private-label where they have no pricing power by definition. So &#x27;pricing power&#x27; as a thesis pillar is - polite version - aspirational. I&#x27;d treat the asset as a price-taker on the volume that matters.&quot;</p>
<p>Sell-side framing of Aurora as price-setter is not supported by the value-chain analysis. Treat as price-taker for modeling.</p>
